-<h1>Chapter 001 — Binary: The Language of Every Computer</h1>
-<h2>Gold Chapter</h2>
-<p>&gt; The purpose of education is not to fill a mind with facts, but to train it to think.</p>
-<h2>A letter to the reader</h2>
-<p>Welcome to Wonderland Academy.</p>
-<p>This chapter is the cornerstone of the Academy. It does not merely introduce binary; it introduces the way Wonderland Academy teaches engineering: from first principles, through practical systems, into real platform improvement.</p>
-<p>Binary is not just a numbering system. It is an engineering solution to a fundamental problem:</p>
-<p><strong>How can imperfect physical devices produce reliable computation?</strong></p>
-<p>Every subject that follows—Linux, networking, databases, security, artificial intelligence, distributed systems, and Wonderland itself—rests on that question.</p>
-<h2>Part I — The nature of information</h2>
-<p>Information is not the same as its representation.</p>
-<p>A photograph is not information by itself. A song is not information by itself. A program is not information by itself. These are interpretations of represented patterns.</p>
-<p>Computers manipulate patterns. Meaning emerges when hardware, software, and people agree on how those patterns should be interpreted.</p>
-</main>
-</body>
-</html>
